## Current baseline already present

### Teamwork choreography — on work, not yet in main

Implementation is in:
- `internal/agent/teamwork.go`
- `internal/agent/teamwork_test.go`
- Supporting changes in `internal/agent/agent.go`, `internal/session/session.go`, their tests, and `internal/tui/tui.go`.

The implementation has a planner produce a JSON task plan with task IDs, roles, write intent, and `dependsOn` edges. `teamTaskWaves` validates dependencies and builds topological execution waves. Independent tasks run concurrently under configured worker limits. Writer work is isolated; its patch is checked and integrated at the end of its wave. Dependent tasks are not released until a writer patch integrates successfully. Worker status, phases, and summaries are recorded in the session activity history and streamed to the TUI.

Continue by auditing the implementation, edge cases, worker permission ceilings, patch-conflict recovery, cancellation, and session-history restoration. Run the existing teamwork tests through CI. Do not duplicate or replace the existing choreography implementation without first identifying a failing test or concrete defect.

### Permission modes — already present in the main baseline; audit/fix, do not blindly re-merge stale PRs

Current tool-layer constants in `internal/tools/tools.go` are:
- `disabled`
- `read-only`
- `workspace-write`
- `ask`
- `unrestricted` (UI-facing name: `autopilot`)

`NormalizeApprovalMode` accepts legacy aliases. `Workspace.Authorize` enforces disabled/read-only/workspace-write/ask/unrestricted behavior; `ShellContext` separately enforces restricted workspace commands, one-time lifecycle approval, and protection against killing the active Agy process. Agent role restrictions, forced-read-only worker ceilings, file mutation blocks, and teamwork authorization must remain consistent across all entry points.

Important open audit:
1. Confirm the canonical UX. Current settings expose five choices, including `read-only`; the earlier permission proposal described four choices (`disable`, `enable`, `ask`, `autopilot`). Decide whether `read-only` remains a separate supported mode or is removed/treated as an alias. Do not let config, TUI, web settings, slash completion, and runtime disagree.
2. Test every path: model tool calls, direct TUI `!` shell, headless runs, Web UI settings/approvals, web tools, teamwork parent authorization, worker restrictions, and worker-patch integration.
3. Verify ask-mode per-operation approvals and response-scoped allow/deny; verify read-only writes are impossible; verify workspace mode path/symlink boundaries and its shell allowlist; verify autopilot remains unrestricted except the intended lifecycle and active-Agy-process protections.
4. Recheck the interaction between role allowlists and permission modes. An unrestricted parent must not silently lift a worker's scoped role/boundary protections.

PR #19 (permission modes) and PR #10 (runtime budgets) are closed without being merged. Do not force-merge or revive their stale heads. Their concepts are partly represented in the current main baseline; inspect current code and tests before deciding whether any specific missing change needs porting.

